Q: Parsing MT940 &amp; MT950 SWIFT using Prowide A: <p>The part of Prowide that trips people up with MT940 is that getField61() and getField86() return two independent lists, so you lose which :86: belongs to which :61:. Walk block 4 in tag order instead and pair them by adjacency:</p>
<pre><code>MT940 mt = MT940.parse(fin);
List&lt;Tag&gt; tags = mt.getSwiftMessage().getBlock4().getTags();
Field61 current = null;
for (Tag t : tags) {
    if (&quot;61&quot;.equals(t.getName())) {
        current = (Field61) t.asField();
        // statement line: current.getValueDate(), current.getDCMark(), current.getAmount()
    } else if (&quot;86&quot;.equals(t.getName()) &amp;&amp; current != null) {
        String info = t.getValue();  // narrative for the :61: right above it
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>MT950 is the same pattern with the MT950 class, most bank MT950s just have no :86: at all. Two practical notes: pick the pw-swift-core SRU release that matches your message vintage (Prowide cuts one per annual Standards Release), and never put the amounts through Double. Field61 amounts are comma decimal strings, take them into BigDecimal.</p>
<p>Since it is adjacent: if the end goal is normalized data rather than owning a parser, my company runs an HTTP API (Transmute) that takes MT940/MT950 and returns JSON with the 61/86 pairing and decimal handling already done, docs at <a href="https://docs.transmute.403fin.io" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.transmute.403fin.io</a>. The Prowide route above works fine too, it is just a yearly maintenance commitment.</p>

Q: What is best practice for preventing a class attribute from becoming stale data? A: <p>You can label the fields you want to have behavior on setting, or be computed on getting, as <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#property" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>@property</code></a>.</p>
<p>One way to do this is to compute your internal set property when it's first accessed (that is, lazily).</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class AlphabetString:
  def __init__(self, istr: str):
    self._string = istr
    self._alphabet: frozenset[str] | None = None

  @property
  def alphabet(self) -&gt; frozenset[str]:
    if self._alphabet is None:
      self._alphabet = frozenset(this._string)
    return self._alphabet

  @property
  def string(self) -&gt; str:
    return self._string

  @string.setter
  def string(self, istr: str) -&gt; None:
    self._string = istr
    self._alphabet = None
</code></pre>
<p>Now if <code>astr = AlphabetString(&quot;foo&quot;)</code>, you can access <code>astr.alphabet</code> and get the frozenset containing <code>f</code> and <code>o</code>, and it will be cached if you access it again.  But if you reassign <code>astr.string = &quot;bar&quot;</code> and then access <code>astr.alphabet</code> again it will be recomputed.  You're not allowed to try to assign <code>astr.alphabet = frozenset()</code> since that doesn't have a setter.</p>
<p>You also have the choice of computing the alphabet <em>eagerly</em>, in the constructor and in the string setter function.  This simplifies the typing a little bit (the internal <code>self._alphabet</code> is never None) but it could waste some cycles if you're never looking up the value.</p>
